  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 325 PM EDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nThe western edge of lower stratus from the remnants of Isaias is \nslowly creeping towards the western portions of our area. Pockets of \nlight rain and drizzle are still ongoing, especially for places \nalong and east of I-65. Chances for precipitation will continue to \ndecrease from west to east as the afternoon progresses, with dry \nconditions expected across the region by sunset this evening. Upper-\nlevel ridging will build in over the Ohio Valley as the remnants of \nIsaias departs off to the east, setting up a period of calm \nconditions that will go into the early parts of the workweek. With \ndecreasing winds expected tonight, in addition to residual moisture \nfrom this weekend's rain, chances (40-60%) for dense fog will \nincrease late tonight, especially for areas along and east of I-65. \nAny fog that does develop will dissipate shortly after sunrise, with \nmostly clear skies expected during the day on Monday. \n\nAs the upper ridge shifts eastward across the central and eastern \nCONUS, southerly surface flow and warmer temperatures will return to \nthe region. Highs on Monday will be in the low to mid 80s, \nincreasing to the mid to upper 80s on Tuesday and Wednesday. A few \nisolated places could reach 90, with LREF outputting 20-30% chances \nfor highs reaching 90 for western and central Kentucky. By Wednesday \nmorning, a frontal boundary pushes southward towards central Indiana \nas the upper-level ridging pattern becomes less amplified. Chances \nfor showers and isolated storms will return as the front continues \nto slowly move southward, becoming nearly stationary over the region \nby Thursday. Chances for precip will continue into the latter parts \nof the workweek before a shortwave trough swings through the Ohio \nValley/Great Lakes regions and helps push the front southward out \nof the area by Friday. \n\nSurface high pressure builds in behind the trough, setting up a \nstretch of dry conditions for next weekend. Another thing to monitor \nwill the the considerable cool down in temperatures after this \nfeatures moves through. Decent cold air advection will help result \nin colder than normal conditions both Saturday and Sunday, with \nhighs in the upper 50s/low 60s forecast. The main focus temperature-\nwise will come during the overnight periods Friday and Saturday \nnight, with the potential for temperatures to drop below 40 both \nnights. While chances are not as high for Saturday morning (15-25% \nfrom latest LREF), chances increase considerably for Sunday morning \n(50-70%) that also introduces the potential for our first frost of \nthe season (30-50% chance).",
